sensors.description:
Which integration provides your battery's sensors? BatteryLedger has {count} sensor profiles for {brands} in the [Store]({store_url}) and has looked for their sensors in this Home Assistant (sensors found · battery modules found; ✗ = not found):
{found}

Choosing one fills in the sensors on the next step, where you can still change them.

modules.description:
Found lifetime discharge sensors for {count} individual battery modules (module: rated capacity → battery model, ✗ = couldn't be matched):
{modules}

Modules found / configured per model:
{per_group}

With per-module tracking each module is measured against its own warranty limit, and the most-used module sets the warranty status. These sensors come from the unofficial Sigen Cloud API and can lag or stop; the plant sensor is still used as a fallback. If anything above is marked ✗, check your battery groups before turning this on.

detect_enable.description:
{count} sensor(s) that help identify your battery are turned off by default in their integration:
{entities}

BatteryLedger can turn them on now. The integration reloads about 30 seconds later, then BatteryLedger checks for their readings after 45 seconds, and again 45 seconds later if they haven't all reported, then carries on. They stay on afterwards, even if you remove BatteryLedger later: BatteryLedger doesn't turn them off again. To turn them off, use Settings → Entities.

detect_towers.description:
Found (inverter · inverter model · battery tower: modules):
{towers}

Were these batteries installed together, as one model with one warranty start date and cost? Batteries added later, or of another brand (e.g. iStore beside Huawei), each need their own battery group.

groups.description:
Battery groups in this setup:
{groups}

Each group is batteries of one model with the same warranty start date and cost (not a stack). Change a group's units or warranty start date, remove a group, or add batteries installed later: more modules in a stack, or another stack.

